---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { publicCurl } from '../data/public-query';
import { SEMPODS_VERSION, SEMPODS_RELEASE_URL } from '../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="sempods — your data belongs to you"
  description="Keep linked data in a pod you control. sempods defines an open contract for apps and agents to use it, with Kotlin building blocks for your own implementation."
  schemaType="WebSite"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">open contract · independent implementations · live pods</p>
  <h1>Your data belongs to you</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Your calendar lives in one app, your notes in another, your AI memory in
    whichever assistant you used last. Changing tools should not mean leaving
    your knowledge behind.
  </p>
  <p>
    Put that knowledge in a pod you control. Give it meaning through linked data.
    Let apps and agents come to it with permission. They can change; your data
    keeps its own home.
  </p>

  <section id="try" class="try-block" aria-labelledby="try-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">try it yourself</p>
    <h2 id="try-title">Query a real pod</h2>
    <p>
      A venue in Chemnitz publishes its events from its own pod. Read three
      events with ordinary SPARQL, without an account, API key or SDK:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{publicCurl}</code></pre>
    <p class="evidence">
      The result contains event addresses, names and dates. Follow an event
      address to read its JSON-LD description.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2>One example, three useful properties</h2>
  <div class="proof">
    <h3>Shared meaning</h3>
    <p>
      The query uses schema.org's Event and startDate terms. The event's
      JSON-LD description uses them too. Another app can recognize an event
      without learning a private data format.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="proof">
    <h3>Links you can follow</h3>
    <p>
      An event's URI is its address. Follow it to get the resource, then follow
      its links. Knowledge stays connected beyond the app that created it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="proof">
    <h3>A home outside the client</h3>
    <p>
      A venue website, calendar app and agent can read from the same pod.
      Each can build its own view while the venue keeps the source data.
    </p>
  </div>

  <hr class="divider" />
  <h2>A common way into independent data spaces</h2>
  <p>
    sempods brings HTTP, RDF, JSON-LD, SPARQL and authorization into one open
    pod contract. It defines what clients can rely on while leaving room for
    different stores, software stacks and deployments.
  </p>
  <p>
    Permissions belong with the data. A public visitor can read the event
    title and date; a trusted app may also reach private planning notes.
    The pod enforces that boundary across resource reads, queries and agent
    tools. <a href="/how-it-works">See how the pieces fit</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>AI can use your memory without owning it</h2>
  <p>
    An agent is another client of your pod. It can find relevant resources,
    follow links and work with the knowledge you allow it to see. Its access
    can end while the knowledge stays in your space.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is useful beyond personal memory. Teams, communities and publishers
    can build on the same idea: useful knowledge with its own address,
    meaning and permission boundary. <a href="/use-cases">Explore the cases</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose where you want to begin</h2>
  <p>
    The specification defines the contract independently of any implementation.
    sempods-kotlin supplies client libraries, reusable services and a reference
    composition. Its current release is <a href={SEMPODS_RELEASE_URL}>{SEMPODS_VERSION}</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Build an app against a pod, implement the contract in your own stack, or
    compose a server from the Kotlin modules. The local composition is for
    development and evaluation; supported upgrades belong to a concrete deployment.
  </p>
  <p>
    The project is early and open to scrutiny. The specification is still in
    development, and a conformance suite remains unfinished.{' '}<a href="/roadmap">See what is available and what comes next</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="/start#build">Build an app</a>{' '}
    <a href="/start#implement">Implement the contract</a>{' '}
    <a href="/start#run">Run a pod</a>{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin/discussions">Discuss the model</a>
  </p>
</Base>
